A||||7||ChopTop||Brake Booster Check Valve??||||1||
Z||000000||ChopTop||10-22-2001||10:19 AM||||Theorge: You should also clean or replace the check valve on the brake booster. That valve traps enough reserve capasity in the booster to give 1-2 pedal stomps. If the valve is messed up, and it sound like yours is, then the booster will loose that reserve and go flat the second the engine isn't at full manifold vac.<P>I've referred to the Haynes manual for this and see no mention of a "check valve".  I know the Haynes manual is sometimes lacking, what am I missing?||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GTZ34||10-22-2001||11:52 AM||||Are you looking for the valve???  If so it is on the booster itself where the vacuum line connects.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-22-2001||07:31 PM||||It's the plastic elbow where the vac line connects to the booster. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||ka4nkf||10-23-2001||10:57 PM||||I would also like to mention that there is a brake booster filter located on the firewall and it is made of plastic. This is a good place for a vacuum leak as it gets hot in this area and the plastic cracks. I really don't think this filter is necessary and I see no reason why you can't cap it off. Also it is hard to get to it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierohoho||10-24-2001||10:43 AM||||You can't just cap the filter off but you can remove it and replace it with a short tube.<P>I lost power assist with my bakes after one pedal pump and if the car sat for a minute it would then be OK for one more pump.<P>Turns out the filter was so plugged it would take forever to pull a vacuum at the booster.<P>Replaced the filter and it now works fine.<P>Any booster problems you have, that fliter bypass would be the place to start the diagnosis.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lightbar.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||tomcat06085||10-24-2001||01:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierohoho:<BR><B>You can't just cap the filter off but you can remove it and replace it with a short tube.<P>I lost power assist with my bakes after one pedal pump and if the car sat for a minute it would then be OK for one more pump.<P>Turns out the filter was so plugged it would take forever to pull a vacuum at the booster.<P>Replaced the filter and it now works fine.<P>Any booster problems you have, that fliter bypass would be the place to start the diagnosis.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Does this problem exist on all Fiero's and, specifically, if an 88GT never really had brakes like you would expect, would this be the first thing to check...after that, in what order....or would an all around system check be in order first.<P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Thanks for the help!||||reg||5
Z||000006||ka4nkf||10-24-2001||02:13 PM||||Fierohoho<BR>Sorry about that I did not mean to cap the line off. just get rid of the filter with a short tube.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-24-2001||07:27 PM||||That I'm aware of.... 87-88 cars don't have the filter in the booster vac line. I'm not sure what older ones do. If it's there you'll find it quick enough just follow the line thru the engine bay.<P>To Recap,,,<P>The booster could be bad. If so it has to be replaced or rebuilt. Rebuilding that is Not something most people should do at home.<P>Booster check valve could be bad or full of crap blocking it open. These can be replaced. they rarely go bad so you can even grab a suitable used one of most U.S. cars made in the last 20+years. Most of the common ones are in the MotorMite catalog. the check valve will screw with the booster operation but it won't cause stalling.<P>The booster vac line could be bad/damaged. Most likely in the engine bay since the problem comes and goes.<P>Since this is an intermitant problem you are going to have to play with it.<P>Here's a couple tests that may help...<P>Park far away from every thing.<P>Block the wheels. all 4 wheels in both directions.<P>Start the car and hold the brakes.<P>Load the engine forwards and backwards with the transmission. Easy with an auto. Be carefull you don't burn the clutch with a stick.<P>If there's a crack back there opening up under various engine loads, that should help find it. The crack could be in either the rubber or the steel line.<P>Next:<P>with the car running, out of gear but the wheels still blocked.... Or in park if it's an auto...<P>Keep pressing and releasing the brake. If the booster is intermitant then this may help you find it. The booster has internal valves that can hang up.<P>If you find that the crack opens with engine load also check and if need be replace the engine/tranny mounts and dog bone. If those are bad they can cause damage to that line and other things.||||reg||1
